feat 1.0

# SPEC-DRV-002 — derive resolves each prediction over the services the spec observes (ADR-0033 draft). Founder ruling
# 2026-11-20 N3 "Per-spec services" (NOT the recommendation; flmnt workspace bc674142, decision f2f873a8): "Feature's
# grammar lets a spec declare the subset of configured services it observes." DRAFT 2026-11-20, awaiting the founder's
# flip. Drafted on the recommendation of open question F3-a (DESIGN.md): `observes` narrows what a success or an error
# predicts and never weakens the language's two absolute guarantees. A rejection still has zero effects on EVERY
# configured service and a query still writes to none: derive synthesises `has []` on each service the spec does not
# observe, for those two, exactly as it already does for every service of a query (ADR-0011). Every service stays set
# up and captured, so the harness and the emitted file do not change (the alternative, F3-b, would need a harness spec).
#
# Driven through packages/feat-derive/fixtures/probe.mjs, a new export `deriveServices` beside `deriveSeeds`:
# `DeriveServices { spec, config, deriveConfig? }` parses the spec against `config`, derives it against `deriveConfig`
# (default `config`; a different one stands for a tier whose services differ from the one the spec was parsed with)
# and answers `observes` (the topology's, comma-joined, absent when none) and `cases`: [{ name, services, implicit }],
# `services` the comma-joined keys of the case's resolved prediction and `implicit` those whose `has []` derive
# synthesised rather than read from the spec (empty when none). A coded error derive throws is answered ERR
# { code, detail }; an uncoded one DERIVE_THREW, as in SPEC-DRV-001.
# Fixtures in packages/feat-derive/fixtures/observes/: config.json (services cache, eventStore, log, all memory, strong;
# command DoThing); all.feat (no observes; a success predicting all three); subset.feat (`observes log, eventStore`;
# a success, a rejection NOPE and an error BROKEN, each predicting eventStore and log); query.feat (type query,
# `observes cache`, response only); two.config.json (services cache and eventStore only).

spec SPEC-DRV-002 "Derive resolves each prediction over the spec's observed services"
context Toolchain
aggregate Derivation
type command
status draft

construct:
  handler at packages/feat-derive/src/index.ts
  touches packages/feat-derive/src/index.ts
  touches packages/feat-derive/derive-observes.feat
  touches packages/feat-derive/derive-observes.contract.json
  touches packages/feat-derive/fixtures/**
  touches feat.config.json
  needs SPEC-CORE-001
  needs SPEC-DRV-001
  The topology carries `observes` from the IR, as written, when the IR has one. A spec with no `observes` derives
  exactly as today. With one, resolvePrediction (in config order, as today):
  a `success` or `error` prediction resolves the observed services only, as written;
  a `rejection` resolves the observed services as written and every other configured service as a synthesised
  { records: [] }, so a rejection still proves zero effects on every configured service;
  a query resolves every configured service as a synthesised { records: [] }, as today, keeping a `contains` on an
  observed service.
  An `observes` key the derive config does not configure is refused UNKNOWN_SERVICE_KEY, an Error whose `code` is set
  and whose message names the key, never a silent drop. The generated file and the harness are unchanged: every
  configured service is set up and captured, and the harness diffs the services the resolved prediction names.

enforce:
  without observes every case resolves every configured service, as before
  a success or an error resolves the observed services only
  a rejection resolves every configured service, the unobserved ones as a synthesised has []
  a query resolves every configured service as a synthesised has [], as before
  rejects UNKNOWN_SERVICE_KEY when observes names a service the derive config does not configure

contract:
  input DeriveInput
  response DeriveResult
  error DeriveError

scenario "a spec with no observes resolves every configured service":
  when: DeriveServices { spec: "packages/feat-derive/fixtures/observes/all.feat", config: "packages/feat-derive/fixtures/observes/config.json" }
  predict success:
    response OK DeriveResult { cases: [{ name: "does the thing", services: "cache,eventStore,log", implicit: "" }], observes: absent }
    filesystem has []

scenario "a spec that observes two services: a success and an error resolve those two, a rejection every service":
  when: DeriveServices { spec: "packages/feat-derive/fixtures/observes/subset.feat", config: "packages/feat-derive/fixtures/observes/config.json" }
  predict success:
    response OK DeriveResult {
      observes: "log,eventStore",
      cases: [
        { name: "does the thing", services: "eventStore,log", implicit: "" },
        { name: "refuses the thing", services: "cache,eventStore,log", implicit: "cache" },
        { name: "fails the thing", services: "eventStore,log", implicit: "" }
      ]
    }
    filesystem has []

scenario "a query that observes one service still writes to no configured service":
  when: DeriveServices { spec: "packages/feat-derive/fixtures/observes/query.feat", config: "packages/feat-derive/fixtures/observes/config.json" }
  predict success:
    response OK DeriveResult { observes: "cache", cases: [{ name: "reads the thing", services: "cache,eventStore,log", implicit: "cache,eventStore,log" }] }
    filesystem has []

scenario "an observes the derive config does not configure is refused, never dropped":
  when: DeriveServices { spec: "packages/feat-derive/fixtures/observes/subset.feat", config: "packages/feat-derive/fixtures/observes/config.json", deriveConfig: "packages/feat-derive/fixtures/observes/two.config.json" }
  predict rejection UNKNOWN_SERVICE_KEY:
    response ERR DeriveError { code: "UNKNOWN_SERVICE_KEY", detail: matching "'log'" }
    filesystem has []
